Getting started with an f8 softcore on a GateMateA1-EVB board


The hardware

The GateMate1-EVB board features the relatively (compared to many cheap boards) powerful GateMate CCGM1A1 FPGA. It is a free board design by Olimex, and substantially cheaper than other boards efaturing this FPGA. We use it together with the LED/button PMOD from the ICEBreaker board.


The toolchain 

* SDCC (4.5.0 or later)
* sv2v (0.0.12 or later)
* Icarus (12.0 or later)
* Yosys (0.38 or later)
* p_r
* OpenFPGALoader

Most of these are available as packages in OS distributions; if any of the tools is missing or tool old on in your distribution, compiling from source isn't hard. p_r is the non-free P&R tool from CologneChips, which can be downloaded from their website.


The f8 softcore and sample programs

We will use a multicycle f8 implementation currently hosted at SourceForge, get via

svn checkout svn://svn.code.sf.net/p/sdcc/code/branches/f8/f8 f8

(or use git-svn if you prefer git)

First, run some pre-synthesis tests:

cd f8/hardware
make tests2

In the end, this should output a list of lines like "testsystem2: tests/clr/test2.log", but not "testsystem2: tests/clr/test2.log ERROR: TRAP".


Blinky

Ensure the LED/button PMOD is at the PMOD2 connector (i.e. the default configuration of a freshly-bought ICEBreaker).
Attach the FPGA board to a USB port.
Create an FPGA image and write it onto the board via

make tests/cblink/gatematea1evb2_synth_00.cfg.bit
openFPGALoader -c dirtyJtag tests/cblink/gatematea1evb2_synth_00.cfg.bit

This should result in seconds being counted (0 to 31) on the 5 LED of the LED/button PMOD (actually at the time of writing this tutorial it counts twice as fast - need to investigate why). The f8 can be reset via the FPGA_BUT1 button on the board. The source for the f8 program is tests/cblink/test.c: The timer is set up for a 1 kHz timer interrupt, and the interrupt handler increments the value on an I/O port once per 1000 interrupts.


Hello, world!

Connect a TTL level serial cable (e.g. a USB-serial converter) to the UEXT1 connector (GND to pin 2, RX to pin 3).
Attach the FPGA board to a USB port. Use a terminal program (auch as gtkterm) configured for the serial port at 9600 baud (actually you should set it to 19200 baud,a s for some reason the board is currently running at twice the speed it should), 1 stop bit, no parity.
Create an FPGA image and write it onto the board via

make tests/chello/gatematea1evb2_synth_00.cfg.bit
openFPGALoader -c dirtyJtag tests/chello/gatematea1evb2_synth_00.cfg.bit

The text "Hello, world!" should be visible in your terminal program (and appear each time you reset the f8 via the FPGA_BUT1 button on the board). The source for the f8 program is tests/chello/test.c: The timer is set up for a 9.6 kHz timer interrupt, and the interrupt handler implements an UART in software, which i used to prove the C function putchar. This putchar is used for output to stdout by the ouput functions in the C standard library.


Dhrystone

Use the same setup as for "Hello, world!" above. Create an FPGA image and write it onto the board via

cd benchmarks
make dhrystone/gatematea1evb2_synth_00.cfg.bit
iceprog dhrystone/gatematea1evb2_synth_00.cfg.bit

At the time this tutorial is written, this show a benchmark score of 592 Dhrystones/s (actually, the f8 is running at twice the frequency it should, but this does not affect the reported score, as the clock used to calculate the time passed is also running at twice the speed it should).

